{"candidate_artifact_hash":"sha256:b00eb1e739e5190b7c35c8c5debf1ec0b0757c2437a128cccbe61741bb8ea8ff","declared_rationale":"The candidate proof correctly instantiates the accepted definition of even (claim.even_definition.v1) for a and b, exhibits integers k and m with a=2k and b=2m, algebraically sums to 2(k+m), and observes k+m is an integer. This provides an explicit witness (k+m) showing a+b is twice an integer, so Even(a+b) holds. There are no hidden assumptions or logical gaps relative to the approved definition and formalization.","findings":[{"code":"step_verification_1","detail":"Assume Even(a) and Even(b). By claim.even_definition.v1 there exist k,m in Z with a=2k and b=2m. Then a+b = 2k+2m = 2(k+m). Since integers are closed under addition, k+m is in Z. Therefore a+b equals 2 times an integer, so Even(a+b) by claim.even_definition.v1. The provided witness is k+m. The argument is direct, uses only the accepted definition, and correctly shows existence of an integer witness for a+b, so the proof supports the target claim.","outcome":"supports","referenced_entity_ids":["claim.even_definition.v1","formalization.even_sum.v1"]}],"recommendation":"manual_review","result_type":"finding","schema_version":"2.0.0","target_claim_id":"claim.even_sum.v1"}